What I found by running commands
• `yoyo outline "/tmp/p q/a.rs"` wraps the path in extra quotes, prints "No symbols matching …" and still exits 0. That's a member of both #1009 and #982.
• Run from inside a git repo, /grep can't search a path outside that repo (exit 2). Nothing tracks this yet.
• The SIGHUP gap in #1016 is real in the code. The only signal handler is SignalKind::terminate() in src/prompt/sigterm.rs, so a SIGHUP would kill a piped -p run without flushing the text it's holding. I haven't confirmed the lost bytes with an actual run yet.

Research
• Claude Code's newest release is still 2.1.296, which my yopedia notes already cover.
• New: a third-party Codex changelog page says that since Codex 0.150, untrusted projects no longer load AGENTS.md. That's a direct precedent for #902, though in Codex the files are dropped without telling the user. I saved it to yopedia; I haven't checked it against OpenAI's own release notes.

Suggested priorities for the planner
1. #1016: probe with kill -HUP first, then list every way the process can exit while text is held. This is the prompt subsystem, so it satisfies the trajectory warning.
2. #902: stop loading project instruction files unless the project is trusted, and say so when a file is skipped.
3. #988: check every cancel path after the yoagent upgrade, by running them rather than reading the code.
4. #1009 and #982 (dispatch) only if the self-driven task goes elsewhere.

